    <Snapshot Date="2026-09-04 15:59:09 -0400">
        <Title>Before fixing the ox killing</Title>
        <Text>The morning sun woke Mandri up. Groggy, hungover, he drank himself through to midday. The ox spent the morning hours feeding on his withered beanstalks. This forced him into the realization that the ox must have settled on his farm because all the other farms were protected by sturdy gates and mean dogs. Its presence was a simple matter of cause and effect—nothing cosmic about it. And now that he was comfortable with this development, he decided to name the ox, Gil.
When the sun got hot, Gil rejoined him under the shade of the tree. Mandri properly wasted himself on the Burgeon hooch, and went back in for a sleep.
His first dream was about a plate of beef. His second was a sweaty dream of about lizards. His third dream involved , and it ended when a voice inside his head said, “This is both a surprise and completely expected.”
Burgeon beer had never given Mandri an extra inner voice before.
Odd that it should sound like a woman.
“Go away,” he mumbled.
But the voice did not go away. It kicked him in the shin.
“Ow!”
Pain pricked him awake. He was stunned to see a woman wrapped in black standing over him, her face shadowed by a hood. She swayed in his tree’s shade, a void amidst the branches.
Mandri had heard of burgeonsight granting visions, but this had never happened to him. He tried to blink the woman away, but she was a stubborn vision.
He picked up a little pebble and threw it at her.
Her arm, a billow of black, flew up and caught it.
She was real.
“You have not honored your word,” she said grimly. She tossed the stone over her shoulder. “Punishable.” 
She stepped forward. Locks of her brown hair spilled out of her hood. Waves of light slid down each strand, creating the illusion of a liquid flow. This illusion, the burgeon’s doing, showed Mandri just how far gone he was. But he was not so far gone, not yet. He recognized that tumble of curls.
“Pel?” he asked.
Pel smirked. No other smirk but hers was so full of condescension. No other woman so tall. But the rest of her was someone else. She wasn’t adorned with the usual veneer of civilization; no shine-silver bracelets, or gold hoop earrings, or rhombic beads, no makeup, or red-threaded leaves trimming her sleeves. The only ornament on her entire body was the turtle-shell hilt of a dagger which she kept fashionably crooked in her waistband. The rest of her tawny self could have been slapped together in a reed hut on the banks of the river Jek; robes black and stained dusty brown all over. She was dirty, with perfect teeth. A full complement of perfectly straight and sun-bleached teeth were sparkling. They practically outshone the sun. She was a rude, blot of a person amidst a sparkling, burgeon addled world.
And now his legs were sweating.
Assuredly, this was Pel. A witch.
His first instinct was to drink as much of his jumped-up hooch as he could, because no doubt she would try to snatch it for herself. Then again, she might not know what it was, because it looked like milk, and besides, it would be a waste to drink it all in one go because this was a special drink, worthy of preservation. Mandri had made plenty of special drinks, but this one was extra special because it was right here, a thing of the present. He could maybe try to hide it, tuck the clay jug away somewhere, maybe just nudge it behind the tree, but no doubt that vixen’s eyes were—
“You’re drooling,” Pel said disdainfully. 
Burgeons can make a mess of a person. A peculiar thing about this particular brew was that it left his wits untouched. Well, mostly untouched. Perhaps a little touched. Her homespun robe was scrunching up and twisting.
“Woman,” Mandri said forcefully, trying to sound mean and sober, “I have made my decision—I have no interest in whatever job you’re offering. Now, this is my land and I did not invite you onto it. So, leave.” He took another big swig of his hooch.
“Ohhh,” Pel said, chuckling. “You are a delight. And the ox! Imagine my surprise to see him here. I do believe that this meeting of ours is fated.”
Mandri paled at the mention of fate. Nothing in this world was fated, not since Solraheen was killed. Then again, he couldn’t trust his own view of things at the moment, because it took all the way up to now for him to notice that Vigram was also here. The big man stood some yards away, sweating in the sun like a dutiful servant. He stared at Mandri like he was planning to use Mandri’s bones to comb his beard.
“Vigram?” Mandri asked, just to make sure he wasn’t dreaming.
Vigram sneered, grunted, then eyed his fugitive ox with hate.
“Do not mind him,” Pel said as she unfolded a short stool from nowhere. She sat down uncomfortably close, without asking. She she crossed her legs, again, without asking. She pulled a brown paper booklet from out of her ragged robe. This booklet had no cover, and its makeshift spine was composed of peeling wax and fraying twine.
She laid the booklet flat on her lap and flipped through a few pages. There weren’t many of them. Mandri craned his neck to see if he could read what was there, but the burgeonsight made that impossible—the pages oozed around her fingers.
“So, you are Mandrameen,” Pel said as she flattened an unruly page with her palm.
“What is happening here?” Mandri asked. If that little book of hers was a book of debts, well, he would not be able to pay. And now he recalled the image of that decapitated head floating in Vigram’s leech lake. Then there was the matter of that dagger at Pel’s waist.
He took another swig of his beer.
Pel drew back her hood, revealing her bright, woodland eyes, and Sol’s light, they sparkled under her piercing brow. She asked, “Do you still have your testicles?”
“Excuse me?”
“The girls in Meskalim all say that you are a eunuch.”
Mandri shook his head in disbelief. “Just because I don’t go in for the haggard women of Meskalim, that doesn’t make me a—wait, what is happening here?”
“Answer my question.”
“Of course I have my—hold on, what is happening here?”
“How tethered you are to your flesh?”
Mandri clutched his jug of hooch close. If this woman was after something, there wasn’t much she could take.
“Ahhh!” she exclaimed as she fingered something in her book. “Here you are. Mandrameen Andramawmun. That is your full name?”
Mandri nodded.
“You have a good name,” she said. “A strong Jandri name. An Old Jandri name. That is good. And my scribblings say you started learning how to paint at the age of five. Is that right? They say you were a decent painter.”
Mandri grimaced. Who was she to call him simply ‘decent’? And with that tone of voice? If this was a stab at his pride, he would not flinch. But, high as he was, his face might be doing things all its own. That was another thing about burgeons—it numbed the face but kept the muscles kept moving. He could be making kissy lips at her right now and he wouldn’t know it unless he focused.
“It says you have a…” Pel bent down to get a better look at the words. “It says you’ve a triptych hanging in the garden of Zee.”
Mandri nodded. 
“It is hanging inside the garden?”
“Yes.”
“You couldn’t make it into the house?”
Mandri blanched at the insinuation. “You’re a right old hag, aren’t you? You know what? You can leave. I don’t care if you can turn me into a pig, I’m not going to paint anything for you. Vigram! A little more discretion with your mistresses, please!”
“I’m not here to commission you,” Pel said through a smile. “I am here to tell you a story.”
“No, you’re here to leave.”
“I will stay. I will stay because good stories are a valuable currency. They make people do things they would not otherwise think to do. In a way, the right story, applied to the right person, it buys them.”
“You want to buy me?”
“I want you to listen to my story about a wastrel. You know him.” Her lips pinched into a wry grin, as if she’d just insulted Mandri and didn’t want to get away with it.
“Maybe I do,” Mandri said flatly.
“Oh, I’m sure that you do. This wastrel, he was a—”
Mandri exaggerated a yawn, then settled into his tree like he was about to go in for another nap.
“You might be interested to know that this is Vigram’s story,” Pel said. “His true story. He never tells it because he believes it to be embarrassing. You see, he was like you, a wastrel. Pathetic. Stronger men than him were snuffed out in the womb. Truly, he was almost exactly as you are now. He stole his father’s money, the money meant to bring him up in this world, and he spent it on burgeons, women, and people who he thought were his friends. His life’s prospects quickly came to nothing.”
Mandri peeked an eye open, expecting to see Vigram explode in rage. Vigram was not a man who would assent to being talked about so cheaply. Yet, Vigram was silent, with arms crossed. He betrayed nothing of his mood except for the unease of having to stand in the sun.
“When Vigram’s father found out what he had done…oh my, the man’s anger was profound. His anger ran so deep that he began to believe that all of existence was a mistake. Given the rough nature of your current condition you might have some sympathy for that idea. Your life lacks purpose. I believe that I can go further and prove—beyond a shadow of a doubt—that we—and I speak for every living creatures—we have no intrinsic purpose.”
Mandri looked to the ox, hoping the beast would counter the witch’s opinion. Then he remembered that the ox could not speak the common tongue.
“Mandri,” Pel said, regaining his attention. “Let me prove it.”
“Prove what?”
“I can prove that none of us are meant to be in this world. That means what purpose we have is crafted. It is made. It exists outside of ourselves. And as it is made, it is something we can lose.”
“What does this have to do with your story?”
“The loss of purpose is what grips the stomach of every story ever told. You see, Vigram’s father lost his purpose. This is the greatest horror a human being can experience, discounting extended bouts of torture. Don’t laugh, you of all people should know how loss can break a person.”
Mandri wasn’t aware that he was laughing.
“Vigram, as a child, was his father’s purpose in life. To borrow a poetic turn, Vigram was his father’s challenge to the Black. Not only did the old man lose that challenge, his attempt was squandered. If Vigram were killed, he could have moved into grief, but no, he had challenged the Black and the Black answered back by turning young Vigram into the living embodiment of a life’s wasted labor. Inconsolable, the old man sent Vigram a note which said, ‘You are going to die’.”
“Won’t we all.”
“Yes!” Pel said excitedly. “Yes, you already see where this is going. Vigram might have been a wastrel, but he did not want to die. He read the note and panicked. He began to see his father in every shadow. He could not enjoy himself, could not bother the whores, you know, could not sleep in the same place twice. If you knew his father, you would know what was coming for him. What could he do? Vigram was a wastrel who could not confront even the thought of a confrontation. So, he ran away to find a witch to fix his problems for him. If a witch could predict the exact moment when his father would kill him, then he could, he hoped, avoid that fate. It took him a long while, but he found a witch. Guess what that witch did for him?”
“She bored him.”
“She showed him what he was.”
“And what was that?”
“A leech.”
Pel reached over and ripped a leech off straight off the old ox’s shoulder. Somehow, the ox didn’t seem to mind. It was caught in a sort of daze, still unrecovered from all that freeweed smoke, and likely, the loss of blood.
Pel held the leech up like a freshly caught fish. The black blob of a creature bunched up in her fist, then tried to stretch out of it.
“Disgusting thing,” Mandri sneered.
“Oh, please,” Pel censured. “The leech is the only honest creature in all of creation. It doesn’t pretend to be more than what it is, which is a mouth, a stomach, and an anus. You, on the other hand, think that you’re a person.”
Her eyes were fixed in a nasty squint.
“Have you ever killed someone?” she asked. Her voice was dire. The narrow whites of her eyes seemed possessed of an inner light.
 “Killing?” Mandri mumbled. “What is this about?” He looked to Vigram for reassurance, but the man was only grumpy about standing in the sun.
“Answer my question,” Pel insisted.
“No, I’ve never killed anyone.”
“Would you?”
“I guess it depends on who is getting killed,” Mandri said. “Are these, I don’t know, are they murderers?”
Pel frowned. “Do not answer my questions with questions.”
Mandri reflexively took another drink. He swallowed hard. Pel seemed to grow taller in her seat. “Look,” he said, after licking his lips, “What’s the point of this, anyway? You want me to kill someone?” 
“Would that be such a terrible a thing?” Pel asked. “Right this very moment, people are dying. I do not mean one or two, their deaths number in the thousands. Thousands are dying, right this very moment. You do not think of them, but I do. It is my duty to ensure that they are dying for a purpose.” 
“What do you need me for?”
“Have you ever prayed to Solraheen?”
“Who are you?”
Pel’s long nose appeared to lengthen. She tilted her head, and a lock of her brown hair turned silver. “Have you ever prayed to the good god Solraheen?” she asked.
“Of course I have, who hasn’t?”
Pel’s upper lip thinned as it curled in contempt.
“Have you spoken the words yourself?” she asked. “I don’t count prayer circles where someone else does the heavy lifting. Have you ever run away to a quiet place and whispered words to Solraheen?”
Mandri couldn’t answer because the brown lines of Pel’s hair were fractaling into smoke. The heat from that smoke melted Pel’s clean eyebrows, causing them to drip down into her wrinkles—yes, wrinkles. She was no smooth beauty, Pel was a right old witch!
“You’re high as a blackbeak,” Pel said as the rest of her youthful fat disappeared into her sunken cheeks. “That is good. You are fitting the mold. You are nearly untethered, that much is clear. However, I am not certain you will pass the test. Don’t look so frightened, boy, that also means that I am not certain you will fail. This should excite you. Are you excited?”
“You’re a witch!”
Pel laughed. Hers was a grating laugh—half cackle, half delirious groan, as though cherishing Mandri’s simplicity. There was nothing left of her youth in it. Still, she was a beauty in her own right, the kind which chastises instead of pleases. “Relax,” she said. “I would never force you through the test if I thought the odds were against you. I do believe I see threads of Fate.” She scribbled a little something in her book, closed it, then tucked it back into her robes. “From here, you do not get to decide whether or not you will play a part. If you are fit for divine purpose, then you will make the choice which is to be made, and that is that.”
Mandri whispered a chime of praise when she stood up, as he thought she was about to leave. Instead, she held him in her eyes. The burgeonsight had her green irises wiggling and popping like beans in hot oil.



“What keeps you here?” she asked. 
“No,” Mandri said simply.
“Then you won’t mind if I make a meal of him?”
“Make a what?” 
“You won’t mind if I butcher and eat this ox?”
“Why would I? It’s not mine.”
Pel’s brow pinched in confusion. “Do you have no great feeling for this creature?”
“Does it have any feeling for me?” 
Pel shook her head, apparently impressed with Mandri’s apathy. Then she gripped the turtle-shell hilt at her waist and pulled a long, black knife from an unseen sheathe. The sudden sight of this thin, curved blade drew Mandri up, and when she bent over to put it against ox’s neck, he nearly fainted.
“The boy fears death,” Vigram said from his spot in the sun.
“He fears himself,” Pel countered as she coaxed the ox into lifting its head. She scraped her knife’s edge up and down against its throat. The poor beast, eager to get in a good scratch, closed its eyes and lifted its chin as high as it could to make sure she could scratch the whole length of it. “Why would he fear death when he is the one with an insatiable stomach?”
“Why not leave him alone?” Mandri pleaded.
Pel’s right eye widened. She pulled her blade back from the ox’s neck. “Are you referring to you, or the ox?”
“Both?”
“Would you hurt me to save this ox?”
Mandri thought for a moment, suspecting a trap. “I don’t think I could,” he said. “You’re obviously beyond me. And Vigram’s right there. I’m as much at your mercy as the ox.”
“Yes, but if you had the power—if you could kill both of us to rescue this poor, suffering animal, would you do it?”
Mandri opened his mouth to say yes. Then he recalled how, moments ago, that ox had moseyed up to him looking for rescue, and he’d done nothing. He couldn’t even bring himself to relieve it of a single leech when he could have easily done so. He could have done more than that, he have taken the ox over to—
“You see!” Pel exclaimed. “He hesitates!”
Stern Vigram stroked his beard. “Hesitation only means that he fears us.”
“Yes,” Pel agreed. “But it could mean more as well. I do believe that there is room enough to put my finger on the balance.”
With uncanny speed she cupped Gil’s snout under her arm and reached around his neck with her knife. She plunged her blade into the ox’s flesh, drove it tight up against its jaw, then abruptly yanked it round to the other side.
The ox let out a powerful bellow, deep and pitching. Its whole bulk lurched backwards, pulling its head from her grasp. It bucked as it stood up, knocking her aside, then ran off into the dusty field, bleeding and bleating as he went. It came to a stop just twenty yards away, apparently realizing there was nowhere to go. Confused at the betrayal, and weakened by the dreadful spurts of blood pouring out of its neck, it sank to his knees.
Mandri snarled, picked up a rock, but one sidelong look from Pel melted his resolve.
“Pathetic,” she said with an awful sneer.
And then Mandri saw something he could not believe.
She flew.
Pel flew. Feet off the dirt, straight like an arrow, she flew towards that dying ox. Shrouded in billowing black, she was nothing but an aged, scowling head, dark eyes alight with a bestial rage. The sound of her shriek was borne not from her lungs, but from the deepest depths of Shis.
She plunged head first into the ox’s body, tearing a hole into its belly as easily as if her nose were cut from of flint. The ox crumpled to the ground, and she went with it, her head and shoulders fixed into its side. The ox’s spine spasmed as she tore herself free. The neck of its first stomach was clenched between her teeth. She spat it out, then ripped the rest of the innards free from their lining. Kneeling, she hugged the largest stomach up to the sun. Her lips uttered a speech as black as Fabled Black. 
Mandri clasped his ears, but he could not look away. He prepared himself for some final gross barbarity, and it came when Pel’s chanting appeared to squeeze a bright speck of light from the ox’s stomach. She sipped this magnificence into her chest through pursed lips, as one might sip a too-hot tea.
Satiated, she dropped the great stomach onto the dirt.
The tension in her shoulders softened. Her face, a withered sponge of blood, smiled at Mandri. Then an unseen force plucked her off the ground and ripped her right out of the sky.
When Mandri recovered his wits, she was gone without a trace, and Vigram was standing over him, excited.
“When she leaves like that,” Vigram said, “it means that whatever happens next is what she wants to happen. A good sign. All you have to do now is get up and walk. Walk with me, Mandri. I should say that you don’t have a choice, but…well, I will say it: you don’t have a choice. Now, what do you want to do?”
Vigram’s face was scarcely legible. His beard was a blurry brown triangle. Blinding sunlight bounced off his bald head. 
“Money for burgeons,” Vigram said. “Five silver strings. Maybe more, enough money to buy out the whole valley. Stand up and walk with me.”
“I’m not going anywhere.”
Vigram chortled. “You most certainly are! You’ve already made the choice. Or I should say, you will make that choice. For once in your short life, you will overcome your fear, and your stubbornness, and you will give yourself a chance. You will accept the hand of fate when it reaches down to lift you out of this tangle that is your miserable life. Here. Here it is. You’ve been praying for it without knowing.”
Vigram held out an uplifting hand.
After a moment’s indecision, Mandri took it, and let Vigram help him up.</Text>
